 2. The internet&#x27;s hidden security nightmare 
 
 By Herb Scribner 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Governments and companies worldwide took websites offline Wendesday after two critical security flaws were discovered in the widely used PHP programming language. Photo: Kirill Kudryavtsev/AFP via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The websites you use every day may have been vulnerable to hackers for years because of hidden flaws in their code that AI only recently discovered.

 Why it matters: The code, called PHP, powers millions of websites, and the vulnerabilities could allow hackers to steal sensitive data like your passwords or take control of affected servers.

 What is PHP? You may have never heard of PHP, even though you've interacted with it for decades.

 PHP, which stands for Hypertext Preprocessor , is a programming language used by roughly 70% of websites whose server-side language is known. It has been around since the 1990s to help build websites, taking data and generating content sent to a browser. It's still used today for a variety of websites, including Wikipedia, WordPress blogs and shopping websites. How it works: HTML determines what a website displays. PHP works behind the scenes to collect information and generate content.

 What was the PHP vulnerability, exactly? AI researchers discovered two critical vulnerabilities in PHP that could allow an attacker to take control of a website's server without having legitimate access to it.

 Hackers could exploit the flaws, dubbed " php2shell " by cybersecurity firms, to steal information from those websites. Between the lines: The flaws went undetected for more than a decade because exploiting them required complex technical work that AI helped researchers discover, per Wiz.

 Why cybersecurity incidents are a big deal Thought bubble via Axios' Bradley Olson : The PHP vulnerabilities are so serious that the NSA warned website operators to take their sites down if they didn't have enough time to patch the bug.

 But the cybersecurity problems in recent days have extended far beyond that and include disruptions to developer platforms, data leaks on public schools, break-ins at public companies and more. A major Axios scoop also revealed that banks are detecting unauthorized third-party agent access inside customer accounts, a problem they are racing to contain. Cyber pros are calling the series of attacks "Black October" due to the sheer volume and the level of risk, although so far many organizations have mitigated the risk and avoided massive real-world harm.

 
 
